import { selectArea } from "/Users/bert/github/sound-map/shared/geocode.js";

const UA = "sound-map/0.1 (https://github.com/bertrandvidal/sound-map)";

// A deliberately mixed bag: big international acts, smaller/older acts, and
// a couple of names MusicBrainz is likely to know only vaguely.
const artists = [
  "Nirvana",
  "Radiohead",
  "Kendrick Lamar",
  "Daft Punk",
  "Björk",
  "Arcade Fire",
  "Fela Kuti",
  "Stereolab",
  "Burial",
  "Godspeed You! Black Emperor",
  "Cocteau Twins",
  "Aphex Twin",
  "The Chemical Brothers",
  "Portishead",
  "Sault",
];

let twoCalls = 0;
for (const name of artists) {
  const res = await fetch(
    `https://musicbrainz.org/ws/2/artist/?query=artist:${encodeURIComponent(`"${name}"`)}&fmt=json`,
    { headers: { "User-Agent": UA } },
  );
  const data = await res.json();
  const artist = data.artists?.[0];
  const area = selectArea(artist);
  const needsSecond = Boolean(area?.id && area.type !== "Country");
  if (needsSecond) twoCalls++;
  console.log(
    `${name.padEnd(30)} area=${(area?.name ?? "none").padEnd(20)} type=${(area?.type ?? "-").padEnd(8)} ${needsSecond ? "2 MB calls" : "1 MB call"}`,
  );
  await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 1200));
}
console.log(
  `\n${twoCalls}/${artists.length} need the second call (${Math.round((twoCalls / artists.length) * 100)}%)`,
);

content.text:
Pipelining is the third lever, and two cheaper ones come first — let me show all three, since the sketch only makes sense against them.

**The floor you're aiming at.** The two locks are independent, so with perfect overlap the cadence is bounded by the busiest one: `max(MusicBrainz 2200ms, Nominatim 1100ms)` = **2200ms/artist**. Today the two stages run strictly back-to-back per artist, and worse, the client's retry after a denial is a flat constant.

**Lever 1 — return the actual remaining TTL.** This is the one I'd do first, and it partly cleans up my own change. Assume ~1s of real work per artist:

```
artist N   starts t=0, takes the MB lock (expires t=2200), responds t=1000
artist N+1 attempts t=1000 → denied
             retryAfterMs = 2200 (today)  → retries t=3200 → cadence 3200ms
             retryAfterMs = 1100 (before) → retries t=2100 → denied → t=3200 → same 3200ms
             retryAfterMs = PTTL (1200)   → retries t=2200 → cadence 2200ms
```

Both constants land in the same place here; my change saved a wasted round-trip rather than costing throughput, but it can overshoot when the work runs longer. Returning `PTTL` on the throttle key when acquisition fails is strictly better than either — the client sleeps exactly until the lock frees. One extra Redis command on the denial path only, and it's ~30% off the cadence.

**Lever 2 — cache the area→parent lookups.** Look again at the sample: London appeared 3 times, Montréal twice, in 15 artists. Areas collide far more than artists do. A `geo:area:<mbid>` key mapping area → parent name means the second MusicBrainz call becomes a Redis hit for every artist from a city you've already seen. That directly shrinks the thing the 2× hold exists to pay for — and it's the change that would make your lease-extension idea worthwhile, because the skip rate stops being ~0%.

**Lever 3 — the actual pipelining.** Client-side, it's a bounded worker pool replacing the sequential loop in `src/useLibraryArtists.js:163`:

```js
const queue = [...initial];
await Promise.all(
  Array.from({ length: CONCURRENCY }, async () => {
    while (isLive()) {
      const artist = queue.shift();
      if (!artist) return;
      await resolveArtist(artist);
    }
  }),
);
```

That's most of the change. `resolveArtist` already owns its throttled-retry loop, `updateArtist` is keyed by id so out-of-order completion is fine, and the counters use functional `setState`. With 2–3 workers, artist N+1 is already queued on the MusicBrainz lock the instant artist N releases it and moves to Nominatim, so the MB lock stops idling — you converge on the 2200ms floor.

What it costs: concurrent workers all retrying on denial means more wasted Vercel invocations (each denial is still a function call plus Redis round-trips), so lever 1 and a bit of jitter are near-prerequisites — otherwise the pool synchronizes and hammers. `SET NX` has no fairness queue either, so which worker wins is arbitrary; harmless at K=2–3, sloppier as K grows. And the abort-on-token-change path (`controller.abort()`) needs to unwind K in-flight requests rather than one — the existing `isLive()` checks cover it, but it's worth a test.

The server-side alternative — one batched request that internally runs an MB stage and a Nominatim stage as concurrent workers over a queue, streaming results back — pipelines more cleanly and wastes no invocations on denials, since a single instance holds both locks locally. Vercel's 300s timeout fits ~50 artists at 2.2s each. But it's a new endpoint shape, a streaming protocol, and a rewrite of the client loop, for the same 2200ms floor. Not worth it unless you want the streaming exercise for its own sake.

My ordering: lever 1 (small, strictly better), then lever 2 (cheap, attacks the real cost), then re-measure — you may not need lever 3 at all. All follow-up work; none of it blocks PR #25. Want me to open an issue capturing this, or just do lever 1 and 2?

